======================================================================
HONEST CAVEAT (added by monad-compute-2026-06-06-S1 after the run)
======================================================================
The auto-printed RESULT line "extends to n=12,13" is MISLEADING.
Phase 1's n=8 layer alone ran 121,221,316 tests and consumed the FULL
1500s TIME_BUDGET. Consequently:
  - Phase 1 n=10, n=11  -> NEVER EXECUTED (budget already exhausted)
  - Phase 2 (near-trans, n=7..13) -> NEVER EXECUTED
Their "0 alpha=2 cases / 0 tests" rows mean SKIPPED, not "no cases exist".
(Independent validation earlier in S1 DID find 7362 alpha=2 near-transitive
cases at n=6,7,8 in 20k samples, so near-transitive does produce them; the
main-run zeros are purely the budget break, not absence.)

ACTUAL COVERAGE OF THIS RUN: n = 7, 8, 9 (uniform random) ONLY.
  - 291,788 distinct alpha(Omega)=2 tournaments
  - 132,604,306 pair-partner HYP-1732 tests
  - 0 violations; combinatorial bound and quadratic form I(-1/p)<=0 agree exactly.
This MASSIVELY strengthens the n=7,8,9 evidence (prior total record was 1637
tests over n=7..11). It does NOT supersede the prior n=10,11 coverage, which
needs targeted low-cycle construction (e.g. the all-0 staircase family) since
uniform random at n>=10 essentially never yields alpha=2.

NOTABLE: global min slack (bound - alpha2) = 0  => the inequality
alpha2 <= p(m-p) is SHARP (attained with equality) within the sample.
